          "type": "output_text",
          "text": "{\n  \"habitat_ai\": \"Infrequently in deciduous and mixed forests, valleys, stream banks, rocky slopes, and cliff edges in temperate mountainous regions; prefers disturbed sites with good drainage.\",\n  \"elevation_ranges_ai\": \"300-1250\",\n  \"ecological_function_ai\": \"Persists in disturbed ecosystems through clonal reproduction, basal sprouting, and aerial roots; provides shade, stabilizes soil, supports biodiversity in remnant habitats; historically dominant in streamside environments.\",\n  \"native_adapted_habitats_ai\": \"Southwestern China (e.g., Zhejiang, Tianmu Mountains, eastern Tibetan Plateau); temperate deciduous forests on acidic loess soils.\",\n  \"agroforestry_use_cases_ai\": \"Urban and street tree plantings for shade and pollution tolerance; fruit production for edible seeds; soil stabilization and erosion control; windbreaks in plantations.\",\n  \"conservation_status_ai\": \"Endangered (IUCN 3.1)\",\n  \"general_description_ai\": \"Ginkgo biloba is a large, long-lived, dioecious deciduous gymnosperm tree, the sole extant species of Ginkgophyta, known as a 'living fossil' unchanged for millions of years; features unique fan-shaped leaves turning bright yellow in autumn, foul-smelling seeds on females, highly resilient to urban stresses.\",\n  \"compatible_soil_types_ai\": \"Well-drained sandy, loamy, or loess soils; tolerant of compacted, clay, poor, acidic to alkaline (pH 4.5-8.5), prefers pH 5.0-8.0.\",\n  \"growth_form_ai\": \"Pyramidal to slender when young, developing broad, irregular oval crown with age; deep-rooted, angular branching, capable of multi-stemmed growth from sprouts.\",\n  \"leaf_type_ai\": \"Fan-shaped, 5-15 cm long, dichotomously branched veins, green on both sides, alternate on long shoots or clustered on short spur shoots; heterophyllous with long-shoot leaves larger and lobed.\",\n  \"deciduous_evergreen_ai\": \"deciduous\",\n  \"flower_color_ai\": \"Inconspicuous; male pollen cones pale green to yellow-green\",\n  \"fruit_type_ai\": \"Fleshy-coated seeds (sarcotesta yellow-brown, 1.5-2 cm, foul-smelling like rancid butter) on female trees; hard-shelled nut inside.\",\n  \"bark_characteristics_ai\": \"Light gray, with shallow furrows and ridges, slightly corky texture.\",\n  \"maximum_height_ai\": 40,\n  \"maximum_diameter_ai\": 5,\n  \"lifespan_ai\": \"Extremely long-lived, typically over 1,000 years, with continuous growth and no senescence.\",\n  \"maximum_tree_age_ai\": 3500,\n  \"stewardship_best_practices_ai\": \"Full sun exposure, well-drained sites; water regularly until established; select male cultivars to avoid fruit litter; low maintenance once mature.\",\n  \"planting_recipes_ai\": \"Plant in spring or fall in full sun, amend heavy soils for drainage; space 10-15 m apart; propagate by seed or graft males onto rootstock; mulch to retain moisture.\",\n  \"pruning_maintenance_ai\": \"Minimal pruning needed; structural prune young trees in spring to develop strong form; remove co-dominant leaders; avoid heavy pruning on mature trees.\",\n  \"disease_pest_management_ai\": \"Highly resistant to diseases and pests; rare issues include aphids, scale, leaf spot; use horticultural oil or insecticidal soap if needed; monitor for canker in stressed trees.\",\n  \"fire_management_ai\": \"Fire-resistant with low flammability, thick bark, and resprouting ability; tolerates low to moderate fires; wood slow to decay.\",\n  \"cultural_significance_ai\": \"Symbol of longevity and resilience in Chinese, Japanese, Korean cultures; planted in temples for millennia; medicinal uses (leaves for extracts, seeds edible); official tree of Tokyo and Seoul; survived Hiroshima bombing; used in bonsai, tea ceremonies, and folklore.\"\n}",
